<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jasna Góra Monastery</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1382">1382</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jasna Góra is a Pauline monastery on a hill in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, founded in 1382 by Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wladyslaw-ii-of-opole">Władysław II of Opole</a>, who brought monks from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. It houses the icon of the Black Madonna. Its successful defence against the Swedes in 1655, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>, made it a national symbol, and it remains Poland's most important place of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jasna-gora-monastery.jpg" alt="Jasna Góra Monastery" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerzy Szota · CC BY-SA 3.0 pl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cz%C4%99stochowa_klasztor_Jasna_G%C3%B3ra-2162.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Jasna Góra, in Polish Luminous Mountain, in Latin Clarus Mons, is a shrine dedicated to the Virgin Mary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czestochowa">Częstochowa</a> and one of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>'s places of pilgrimage. It was founded in 1382 by Pauline monks who came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> at the invitation of Vladislaus II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opole">Opole</a>, and the new monastery was entrusted with the icon of the Mother of God with the Christ Child, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-madonna-of-czestochowa">Black Madonna of Częstochowa</a> or Our Lady of Częstochowa, to which miraculous powers are attributed. It was originally a single-nave church, enlarged around 1463 into a three-nave Gothic hall church. The site is one of Poland's official national Historic Monuments.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 14 April 1430 Jasna Góra was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>. In the winter of 1655 the Swedish army unsuccessfully besieged the monastery during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-northern-war">Second Northern War</a>, or the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-deluge">Deluge</a>. The event stimulated Polish resistance, and on 1 April 1656, in the cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lviv">Lwów</a>, King Jan Kazimierz solemnly vowed to consecrate the country to the protection of the Mother of God and proclaimed her Patron and Queen of the lands of his kingdom. The Swedes besieged the monastery again without success in 1702, 1705 and 1709, and Russians besieged it in 1770-1772 during the Bar Confederation.</p><p>Reconstruction in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> style took place between 1690 and 1693, and between 1693 and 1696 the walls of the nave and chancel were raised, turning the church from a hall into a basilica. In 1717 the icon was crowned by decision of Pope Clement XI. From 1760 to 1772 Jacob Frank, the founder of the Frankist sect, who considered himself a messiah, was imprisoned in the monastery. In 1909 thieves stole jewels, pearls and other valuables worth millions of rubles, though the icon was not damaged. Pope Pius X offered to replace the stolen crown, and a new coronation in 1910 drew crowds of up to 60,000. A monk, Damazy Macoch, confessed to the theft in 1910.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Every year since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> have walked to Jasna Góra in pilgrim groups. In 2011 it was estimated that 3.2 million pilgrims from 80 countries came to the shrine, about 830,000 of them in 228 organised pilgrimages, and the average group travelled about 350 kilometres in 11 days. Visitors are expected to be silent in the monastery, and pilgrims pass in front of the icon on their knees. Among its exhibits is the medal of the 1983 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> awarded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-walesa">Lech Wałęsa</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jasna Góra Monastery” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Władysław II of Opole served as palatine of Hungary and in 1382 founded the Pauline monastery of <b>Jasna Góra</b> at Częstochowa, bringing the Black Madonna icon.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-opole">Duchy of Opole</a></li></ul></section>
